"""Grade 11 life essentials: work, money and calling."""
from spec import L, U

N11 = U('Purpose, money and calling', 'Morality', 'What will I do with my life, and how do I handle the things that run it?', [
L('Work, calling and the question “What will I do with my life?”',
  'Students are asked what they will be, as if that were a job title. The Church gives a bigger frame: every baptized person is called to holiness, work is a way of sharing in God’s creating, and a vocation is the particular shape of that call. This lesson ties together the Catechism on work, the universal call to holiness, the laity’s mission and the state of life, so that choosing a path becomes a question of love and not only of income.',
  '2427-2429,2013-2015,898-900,1533-1535,2428,307',
  'Genesis 2:15;Colossians 3:23-24;Matthew 25:14-30;1 Corinthians 12:4-11',
  'vocation laity stewardship virtue',
  'If money were not a concern, what would you do with a year? What does that say about what you value?',
  'Draw three overlapping circles: what the world needs, what I am good at, what I love. Write two or three items in each. Then add a fourth circle, “what God may be asking.” Pick one question to take to prayer and one person to talk to this month.',
  'for-vocation',
  id='11-L1',
  hook='Ask students to finish the sentence out loud, “When I grow up, I want to be…” then ask: “Is that who you want to be, or what you want to do?” Compare the two.',
  teach=[
    'Start with the biggest claim. “All Christians in any state or walk of life are called to the fullness of Christian life and to the perfection of charity” (CCC 2013). Holiness is not a job for specialists. A mechanic, a nurse, a coder and a priest are all called to be saints, in different ways. So the first question is not “What career?” but “Who am I being, and for whom?”',
    'Work is part of that. In Genesis, God puts Adam in the garden “to till it and keep it” before the Fall (Genesis 2:15). Work is a way humans share in God’s work of creating (CCC 2427). It also serves others: “Work is for man, not man for work” (CCC 2428). Good work has dignity, fair pay, and limits so it does not swallow the person (CCC 2428–2429). Paul says it simply: “Whatever your task, work heartily, as serving the Lord” (Colossians 3:23).',
    'The laity have a particular mission: to “seek the kingdom of God by engaging in temporal affairs and directing them according to God’s will” (CCC 898). That means a Catholic in law, nursing, trades, tech or teaching is not on the sidelines of the Church; they are the Church there. Then comes the state of life. Some are called to marriage, some to priesthood, some to consecrated life, some to a single life of service, and the sacraments of Holy Orders and Matrimony exist “for the salvation of others” (CCC 1534).',
    'Discernment needs more than feeling. Gifts matter: the parable of the talents says each is given something and expected to invest it (Matthew 25:14–30). Needs matter. Counsel matters. And God’s providence works through all of it (CCC 307). A vocation is not a code you crack; it is a conversation you enter. Take the next faithful step and pay attention to the fruit.',
  ],
  talk=[
    'Is “What do I want to be?” the same as “What am I called to?”',
    'How does it change work if it is a way of serving others?',
    'What do you think your gifts are? What do others say?',
    'What gives you a deep kind of tired and a good kind of joy?',
  ],
  quiz=[
    'What is the universal call to holiness?|Only priests and religious are called to be saints|All Christians in any state of life are called to the fullness of Christian life|Holiness is only for the old|It is optional|1|CCC 2013.',
    'What does “Work is for man, not man for work” mean?|Work does not matter|Work should serve the person and not swallow him|People should avoid work|Work is a punishment only|1|CCC 2428.',
    'What is the mission of the laity?|To stay silent|To seek God’s kingdom by engaging temporal affairs and ordering them according to God’s will|To replace priests|Only to attend Mass|1|CCC 898.',
  ],
  life='Take your circles to two people who know you well (a parent, a teacher, a mentor) and ask: “Where do you see me coming alive?” and “What do you think I am good at?” Write down their answers. Spend ten minutes praying with the question, “What do you want of me?”',
  saint='Joseph: the carpenter of Nazareth, who worked with his hands, protected Mary and Jesus, and is the patron of workers.',
  misc=[
    '“A vocation is only priesthood or religious life.”',
    '“God has one secret job for me and I might miss it.”',
    '“Work is separate from faith.”',
  ],
  deeper=['room:desales-devout-life', 'room:lawrence-practice'],
  mem='“Whatever your task, work heartily, as serving the Lord and not men.” (Colossians 3:23)',
  assess='Ask each student to explain the difference between the universal call to holiness and a particular vocation, and to name one next step.',
  script=dict(
    open='Ask students to complete the sentence aloud: “When I grow up, I want to be…” Then say, “Now: who do you want to be, as opposed to what you want to do?” Let them notice the difference.',
    teach=[
      'Say: “The first call is not a career. It is holiness. The Catechism, 2013, says all Christians in any walk of life are called to the fullness of Christian life. So a nurse, a mechanic, a coder and a priest are all called to be saints. The question is not just what you will do. It is who you will be, and for whom.”',
      'Say: “Work is part of this. In Genesis 2, before sin, God puts Adam in the garden to till and keep it. So work is not a punishment. It is a way of sharing in God’s creating. The Catechism says work is for man, not man for work. It should serve people and not swallow them. Paul says, ‘Whatever your task, work heartily, as for the Lord.’”',
      'Say: “The laity have a special mission, number 898: to seek God’s kingdom in the ordinary world and order it according to God’s will. If you are Catholic in a hospital, law firm or workshop, you are the Church there. Then there is the state of life: marriage, priesthood, consecrated life, or single life given to service. Each is a real call.”',
      'Say: “How do you discern? Look at gifts, needs, counsel and prayer. The parable of the talents says each of us is given something and is asked to invest it. A vocation is more of a conversation than a code. You take the next faithful step, then look at the fruit. God is patient and works with imperfect choices.”',
    ],
    ask=[
      'What gives you a good kind of tired?',
      'Whom could you ask where they see you come alive?',
      'What is one next step you can take this month?',
    ],
    practice=[
      'Give each student a sheet with three circles: world needs, I am good at, I love. Fill in two or three items for each.',
      'Add a fourth circle: “God may be asking.” Write one question.',
      'Pair up and name one person you will ask this month, and what you will ask.',
    ],
    close='Pray the vocation prayer together. Say, “You do not need to see the whole path. You need to take one honest step.”'),
  tryit=['Fill in three circles: world, gifts, love.', 'Add one question for God.', 'Name one person to ask this month.'],
  saintStory='Joseph appears in the Gospels of Matthew and Luke and never speaks a word that is recorded. He was a descendant of David, from Nazareth, a tekton, a craftsman who worked in wood and probably stone. He was engaged to Mary, and when he found she was with child, he planned to quietly divorce her rather than disgrace her. An angel told him in a dream to take Mary as his wife.\nHe took her to Bethlehem for the census, where Jesus was born. When Herod threatened the child, an angel told Joseph in a dream to flee to Egypt, and he went that night with Mary and the child. After Herod died, he brought them home to Nazareth. The Gospels show him taking Jesus to the temple at twelve, and then he disappears from the story, probably dead before Jesus’ public ministry.\nThe Church honors him as the patron of workers on May 1 and as Husband of Mary on March 19. In 1870 Pius IX declared him patron of the whole Church, and Pope Francis called him a father in the shadows who was strong and tender in the same breath.',
),

L('Your first paycheck: money, debt and being generous when you have little',
  'At seventeen, money arrives all at once: a job, a card, a car, subscriptions, a school loan. No one teaches how to hold it. The Church’s moral teaching on honesty in dealings, promises, temperance, generosity and poverty of spirit has direct things to say to a young person about debt, spending, saving and giving, and about not being owned by what you own.',
  '2407-2414,2409,2411,2413,1809,2443-2447,2536,2544-2547',
  'Proverbs 22:7;Luke 14:28-30;Luke 21:1-4;Matthew 6:19-24',
  'stewardship almsgiving temperance justice',
  'Where does your money go in a typical week, and what does that say about what you love?',
  'Write a one-page “first budget.” Four lines: income, needs, wants, giving and saving. Set a percentage for each (even if the income is small). Pick one subscription or habit to cut, and set a first amount to give away.',
  'before-meals',
  id='11-L2',
  hook='Ask a student to estimate how much a $4 daily drink costs in a year ($1,460). Then ask: “What could that do for someone else?” Let students react without comment. Read Luke 21:1–4, the widow’s two coins.',
  teach=[
    'Honesty comes first. The seventh commandment forbids stealing, but also fraud, unjust wages, and breaking contracts. “Promises must be kept and contracts strictly observed to the extent that the commitments made in them are morally just” (CCC 2410–2411). That applies to a phone contract, a loan, a promise to repay a friend, or the hours you said you would work. Debt is not a sin; careless debt is a trap, and repaying what you owe is a matter of justice.',
    'Temperance is the virtue that “moderates the attraction of pleasures and provides balance in the use of created goods” (CCC 1809). It is freedom: a person who can say no to the third purchase can say yes to something worth more. The tenth commandment targets the opposite: greed, envy and the endless wanting that no purchase satisfies (CCC 2536, 2539–2540). Advertising is built to produce exactly that feeling.',
    'The Church insists that giving is not an extra. “God blesses those who come to the aid of the poor” (CCC 2443), and almsgiving is a work of mercy (CCC 2447). Notice the story of the widow: the gift is measured not by size but by what it costs (Luke 21:1–4). Giving a small fixed share regularly is better than rare large gifts. It trains the heart as well as helping someone.',
    'All of this sits under “poverty of heart,” the first Beatitude (CCC 2544–2547). It does not mean being poor; it means not being owned. A wise young person sets up three habits: a small automatic giving, a small automatic saving, and a rule that every purchase over a certain amount waits 48 hours. These make freedom out of ordinary money.',
  ],
  talk=[
    'What are three ways advertising tries to make you want things you do not need?',
    'What is the difference between a loan you can repay and a debt that traps you?',
    'What did the widow’s gift cost her? What does your giving cost you?',
    'If you gave 5 or 10 percent, to whom would you give?',
  ],
  quiz=[
    'What does the Church say about keeping promises and contracts?|They are optional|They must be kept to the extent they are morally just|Only written ones matter|Only for adults|1|CCC 2410–2411.',
    'What is temperance?|Never spending|The virtue that moderates the attraction of pleasures and balances the use of created goods|Being stingy|Hating money|1|CCC 1809.',
    'What does “poverty of heart” mean?|Having no money|Not being owned by possessions|Being sad|Giving everything away at once|1|CCC 2544–2547.',
  ],
  life='Set up two small automatic habits this month: giving a fixed share (even 2 percent) to a parish or charity you have checked, and saving a fixed share. Add a 48-hour rule for any purchase over an amount you choose. Write the three numbers on a card.',
  saint='Frédéric Ozanam: a French university student who at twenty founded a small group to serve the poor in Paris, which became the Society of St. Vincent de Paul.',
  misc=[
    '“Giving is for people who have extra.”',
    '“Borrowing is always bad.”',
    '“Money is neutral and has nothing to do with faith.”',
  ],
  deeper=['apol:tithing-and-money', 'apol:social-justice-poor'],
  mem='“You cannot serve God and mammon.” (Matthew 6:24)',
  assess='Ask each student to define temperance and poverty of heart, and to name their three money habits.',
  script=dict(
    open='Ask, “If you buy a four-dollar drink every day, how much is that in a year?” Let them guess, then say, “About fourteen hundred and sixty dollars.” Ask, “What could that do?” Then read Luke 21:1–4 aloud.',
    teach=[
      'Say: “Start with honesty. The seventh commandment is about more than stealing. The Catechism lists fraud, unjust wages, and breaking contracts. Promises and contracts must be kept (2410). That includes a phone plan, a loan, a promise to pay a friend back. Paying what you owe is not charity; it is justice.”',
      'Say: “Next, temperance. The Catechism, 1809, calls it the virtue that moderates the attraction of pleasures. It is a freedom: if you can say no to the third purchase, you can say yes to the thing that matters. Advertising is designed to create wanting. The tenth commandment is about that endless wanting. You can see it with a phone ad.”',
      'Say: “Then giving. Number 2443: God blesses those who help the poor. In Luke 21, a widow puts in two coins, and Jesus says she gave more than everyone. Not because of the amount, but because of what it cost. Small, regular giving trains the heart. Set it up before you spend, not after.”',
      'Say: “All of this sits under the first Beatitude: poor in spirit. Not penniless; not owned. Three habits will protect your freedom: a small automatic gift, a small automatic saving, and a 48-hour rule on big purchases. None of this requires a lot of money. It requires deciding in advance.”',
    ],
    ask=[
      'What do you spend on that you do not even enjoy?',
      'What would a small regular gift look like for you?',
      'What is one purchase you could delay for 48 hours?',
    ],
    practice=[
      'Give out a budget card: income, needs, wants, giving and saving.',
      'Set a percentage for each line, even for a small income.',
      'Write the three habits: giving, saving, the 48-hour rule.',
    ],
    close='Pray the grace before meals. Say, “What you have is a gift. Decide ahead of time how to use it.”'),
  tryit=['Fill in a first budget.', 'Set a giving and a saving percentage.', 'Write the 48-hour rule.'],
  saintStory='Antoine-Frédéric Ozanam was born in Milan in 1813 to French parents and raised in Lyon. As a boy, he went through a crisis of faith, and was rescued by a teacher who showed him how reason and faith fit together. At eighteen he went to study law in Paris, at the Sorbonne, at a time when many professors mocked religion.\nIn a student debate, a skeptic told him, “You Catholics talk about what the Church did in the past. What do you do now?” Ozanam took this to heart. In 1833, aged twenty, he and a few friends started a small group to visit the poor in their homes and bring them firewood and food. They named it after Vincent de Paul. Within twenty years the conferences spread around the world.\nOzanam became a professor at the Sorbonne, married Amélie in 1841 and had a daughter. He worked himself to exhaustion, and died in 1853, aged 40. He was beatified by John Paul II in 1997.',
),

L('Vocation: how to hear a call when there is no voice',
  'Most people do not hear a voice. They have a pull, a pattern, a series of doors that open and close. The Church’s teaching on vocation, marriage, priesthood, consecrated life and the single life, together with the tradition of discernment, gives a method for hearing a call within ordinary life. This lesson is also an invitation: to take the question seriously and to bring it to God, to a friend and to a good priest or mentor.',
  '1,1603-1605,1579-1580,914-933,1877-1879,1788,1806,2013',
  'Isaiah 6:1-8;1 Samuel 3:1-10;Jeremiah 1:4-8;Luke 1:26-38',
  'vocation discernment prayer consecrated-life',
  'How would you know if God were asking something of you? What would you want to be true about it?',
  'Make a two-column page: “Signs of a good call” and “Signs it is not mine.” Under the first, write examples of peace, fruitfulness, confirmation from wise people, and fit with your gifts. Under the second, write fear that is only pressure, flight from something, or a wish to impress. Then pick one vocation you have not seriously considered and spend one hour learning about it.',
  'for-vocation',
  id='11-L3',
  hook='Read 1 Samuel 3:1–10, where Samuel hears a voice three times and thinks it is Eli. Ask: “Why did Samuel not recognize God’s voice? What did Eli do to help?”',
  teach=[
    'The first fact is the Catechism’s first sentence: God “has created us to know him, to love him”, and invites us into friendship (CCC 1). A vocation is an answer to that invitation. Everyone has a vocation in the large sense, the call to holiness and love (CCC 2013), and a particular shape that call takes: marriage, holy orders, consecrated life or single life given in service. Each is described by the Church as a real path to holiness (CCC 1603–1605, 1579–1580, 914–933).',
    'Samuel’s story is a pattern. He hears the voice three times and does not recognise it. Eli, an older man, tells him what to say: “Speak, Lord, for your servant hears.” A call often becomes clear with help. Mary is another pattern: she asks a question (“How can this be?”) and then gives her yes (Luke 1:34–38). God does not mind questions; he looks for openness.',
    'Practically, discernment uses what the Catechism calls prudence, advice and the Holy Spirit (CCC 1788, 1806). Look for peace that lasts, fruit in service, fit with your gifts, the confirmation of wise people, and a draw toward love rather than away from fear. Be suspicious of pressure, of mere escape, and of wanting to impress. Expect the call to be known by living it, step by step, not by a decision made once at the top of a mountain.',
    'And do not leave out the question of priesthood and religious life. Vocations to them are urgently needed and joyfully lived. The Church asks all young people to be open to them, not because most will be called, but because the question is a real one and needs a real answer (CCC 1578–1580, 915–916). Marriage is just as much a vocation, and the single life lived for others is as well (CCC 1658, 2013).',
  ],
  talk=[
    'Why do you think God’s call is often unclear?',
    'What is a sign of a good call? What is a warning sign?',
    'Which vocation have you never seriously considered? Why?',
    'Who could play the role of Eli for you?',
  ],
  quiz=[
    'What does the Church say about vocation?|It is only for priests|Everyone is called to holiness, and each lives it in a particular way|It is a job|It is a feeling|1|CCC 2013.',
    'What helps in discernment, according to the Catechism?|Only feelings|Prudence, advice, and the Holy Spirit|Coin flips|Pressure|1|CCC 1788.',
    'What did Eli teach Samuel?|To ignore the voice|To say, “Speak, Lord, your servant hears”|To run away|To wait for ten years|1|1 Samuel 3:9.',
  ],
  life='For one month, pray each day for five minutes with one question: “Lord, what do you want of me?” Spend one afternoon talking with a priest, a sister or brother, a married couple, and a single adult about how they discerned their path. Write down what you hear.',
  saint='Teresa of Calcutta: a teaching sister who heard what she called “a call within a call” on a train in 1946.',
  misc=[
    '“God’s plan is a secret I could miss.”',
    '“If I do not feel a strong calling, I do not have a vocation.”',
    '“Vocation is only for priests and nuns.”',
  ],
  deeper=['apol:celibacy', 'room:desales-devout-life'],
  mem='“Here I am! Send me.” (Isaiah 6:8)',
  assess='Ask each student to describe the main vocations, name two signs of a good call, and say what they will do this month.',
  script=dict(
    open='Read 1 Samuel 3:1–10 aloud. Ask, “Why didn’t Samuel recognise the voice? What did Eli do?” Then say, “Most people don’t hear a voice either. Today is about how a call usually comes.”',
    teach=[
      'Say: “The Catechism begins: God made us to know and love him. A vocation is an answer to that invitation. Everyone has a vocation in the big sense: holiness. Then there are particular shapes: marriage, priesthood, consecrated life, single life given to service. Each is a real way to holiness. None is second-class.”',
      'Say: “Samuel hears a voice three times and does not know it is God. Eli helps. That is a pattern: calls are clearer with help. Mary asks a question and then says yes. God doesn’t mind questions. He looks for openness. So do not wait to feel sure. Be open, ask, and talk to people who have lived it.”',
      'Say: “Signs of a good call: lasting peace, fruit in service, fit with your gifts, confirmation from wise people, and a pull toward love. Warning signs: pressure, escape, or wanting to impress. Discernment is slow. You learn a call by living it, one step at a time.”',
      'Say: “And do not rule things out. The Church needs priests, sisters and brothers, and they are happy. Not many are called, but the question is real. Marriage is a vocation. A single life given for others is a vocation. The question to carry is: ‘Lord, what do you want of me?’ and then listen.”',
    ],
    ask=[
      'Which vocation have you never seriously considered?',
      'Who has lived a vocation in a way you admire? What would you ask them?',
      'What fear gets in the way of listening?',
    ],
    practice=[
      'Hand out a two-column sheet: signs of a good call, signs it is not mine.',
      'Fill in each side from what you heard.',
      'Write one person to interview this month and three questions.',
    ],
    close='Pray the vocation prayer together. Say, “Ask the question every day. The answer comes by walking.”'),
  tryit=['List signs of a good call and signs it is not yours.', 'Choose one vocation to learn about.', 'Write three questions for someone who lives it.'],
  saintStory='Agnes Gonxha Bojaxhiu was born in 1910 in Skopje, now in North Macedonia, to an Albanian family. At eighteen she joined the Sisters of Loreto in Ireland and was sent to India, where she taught at a girls’ school in Calcutta for nearly twenty years and became its principal.\nOn September 10, 1946, on a train to Darjeeling for a retreat, she said she heard Jesus ask her to serve the poorest of the poor in the slums. She called it a call within a call. She did not just act. She wrote to her archbishop, and for almost two years was told to wait and test it. In 1948 she received permission to leave the convent, trained briefly as a nurse, and began to teach slum children, then to care for the dying.\nIn 1950 the Missionaries of Charity were approved. Mother Teresa later learned to live with a long inner darkness, which she kept private. She died on September 5, 1997 and was canonized in 2016.',
),
])
